{
  "title": "Sun mask with glued-on rays",
  "summary": "A sun face mask, 19 cm across, with 16 rays (8 wavy, 8 straight) printed separately and glued around it: 37 cm in all.",
  "status": "preview",
  "date": "2026-10-30",
  "material": "PLA",
  "notes": "Scaled so the eye holes are 64 mm apart (centre to centre): the face is 191 × 192 mm and 57 mm deep, and the whole sun is about 370 mm across, too big for the bed in one piece. Each ray's root is shaped to the face's edge where it is glued, and the backs of the face and all rays lie on one plane. To glue, lay the face and the rays face-up on a flat table, push each ray against the edge, and use epoxy or thick superglue. Plate 1: the face, printed with the open back on the bed, so the organic tree supports stay inside against the wearer. The back rim is trimmed 3 mm flat for a full ring on the bed, plus a 5 mm brim. Plate 2: all 16 rays flat on their backs, no supports, 3 mm brim. The face prints on Gås, the rays on TNT (230 °C, flow capped at 12 mm³/s for its weak hot end). Face: 0.24 mm layers, 3 walls, 4 top/bottom layers and lighter support interfaces (0.8 mm spacing), which brings it from 12 h 16 min at 0.16 mm to 8 h 38 min with the same support coverage. Rays: 0.16 mm layers, 4 walls. Both use 15 % infill (decor). No strap holes or slots yet.",
